nadian women  one in eight women are expected to  develop breast cancer during their  lifetime  and one in 33 will die as a result of  the disease  in general people don't die from the  breast cancer directly from the primary  tumor  but they die from the metastatic cancer  so breast cancer in particular tends to  metastasize to the brain  the lungs the liver and the bone and  this process has a number of different  steps  starting from the primary tumor where  the cells  invade the basement membrane travel  through the tissue  interversate into the bloodstream where  they can travel throughout the body  they can then extrapolate out of the  bloodstream and migrate to  different regions of different organs  and metastasize  historically cancer treatments have  aimed to kill the cancer cells  but if we can inhibit cell migration and  invasion then we have  viable targets for cancer treatments and  potentially new therapeutics  so we really want to to find ways to  inhibit metastases  and in order to do this we need to  understand the molecular mechanisms at  play in regulating these different steps  i'm going to tell you today about two  different  structures and and how the lpp protein  is playing a role in regulating them so  the first structure is the  focal adhesion which is a structure  which links the  cell to its extracellular matrix through  contacts between the cytoskeleton and  integrand receptors that  that bind to the ecm or the  extracellular matrix  and i'm also going to talk about in beta  podia which are  structures which invade perpendicular to  the cell down  into the extracellular matrix and  degrade the tissue and push through so  that the cells  can invade different tissues the  diploma preferred partner lpp is a  protein  that we've been interested in studying  it's 80 kilodaltons and belongs to the  vixen family  it's involved in spatial control of  actin assembly and mechanal transaction  and it interacts with number of  different proteins that both cell cell  contacts and focal adhesions are cell  matrix contacts  it has the proline rich region with an  alpha tin and binding domain that  i'll tell you more about after it  localizes two  adhesions in response to tgf beta growth  factor treatment  and this localization is dependent on  one of the limb domains  lim1  so the reason we've been interested in  lpp is that it's been linked to  metastatic cancer  and this is work done by my  collaborative lab  peter siegel at the goodman cancer  center at mcgill  and what they've been able to show is if  they inject breast cancer cells  into the fat pad of mice they grow  memory tumors  and these tumors grow over time  and this tumor growth is not impacted by  lpp  so if the cells are injected with an lpp  knockdown the tumor growth  is unchanged however importantly the  metastatic burden of the animal is  changed  so in normal conditions with lpp  present the tumor cells metastasized to  the lung and you can see  from these histological stains a huge  tumor burden  in the lung of the animal however when  lpp is knocked down that tumor burden is  much reduced and you see very few  regions in the lung in addition if you  look in the bloodstream  at circulating tumor cells they're quite  prevalent  in controlled situations where lpp is  expressed  but when lpp is knocked down you don't  see very many circulating tumor cells  so lpp is not affecting tumor growth but  it's implicated  in the ability of the cells to get into  the bloodstream and also  to get into organs at sites and  metastasis  we were interested in understanding at  the cellular level how  ltp might be regulating this process  so we looked at lpp role in cell  migration  so these are individual cells which are  plated  on fiber nectar and coated dishes and  we track them over time using bright  field imaging and then we calculate  cell tracts for each cell in the blue  here you see  tracking of cells under normal  conditions and in the red  is the cell movement after growth factor  treatment  and you can see that the cells move  faster and further following the growth  factor treatment  under conditions where the lpp protein  has been knocked down even with growth  factor treatment  the cells cannot increase in their cell  speed so this dependence  on lpp for tgf beta induced  migration it's there in addition  um you can see here the blocks showing  the lpp knockdown  and you can see um that we have this in  enhanced cell migration dependent on the  tgs data treatment  the lpp is also a time dependent process  so you can see that we have this  time lapse data shown here  and as we treat with cgf data you can  see a gradual increase in the cell  migration speed and we reach a plateau  around  15 to 18 hours after treatment  whereas again in the knockdown cells we  don't see any increase in cell migration  over time  so we were interested in looking at in  more detail at the molecular level  what's going on in the cell  so we're interested in looking at cell  adhesion  and this is a movie showing uh cells  expressing a paxillin gfp protein  paxon is an adapter protein that's known  to localize to the adhesion  and you can see that these structures  are very dynamic they're assembling and  disassembling  there's different sizes different shapes  they're  present all across the cell and there's  very tight regulation  both in time and space across the cell  and  how are these structures playing a role  in regulating cell movement  so if we look at the cell when when  cells migrate they basically have to  assemble adhesions and attach to the  extracellular matrix at the front  this usually happens through nascent  focal adhesions that are very dynamic  and can develop into intermediate focal  adhesions that are a little bit larger  and more  well developed and these create an  attachment  to the surface and force can be  generated to pull the cell forward  at that time the cell the adhesions at  the rear of the cell need to disassemble  in order to release the attachment and  allow the whole cell to process forward  so we're interested in understanding  these different structures  and their dynamics  so there's also three-dimensional  organization so this is a cartoon of an  individual one of these structures so if  we look  it would be one of these single  structures in the south and the  molecular organization there  so we have um the integrin which is  linking  the extracellular matrix to the  cytoplasmic side of the cell these are  transmembrane proteins  we have an integrand signaling layer  this is where paxillin and a number of  other proteins are  kinases phosphatases things that are  regulated and controlling downstream  signaling in the cell there's a forced  transduction layer which consists of a  lot of structural proteins such as  vinculin and talin that are linking  the adhesion to the actin cytoskeleton  and then there's an actin regulatory  layer which contains proteins such as  vixen and we believe  lpp would be in this layer since it's in  the vixen family  and just to mention that we're only  showing nine of the over 200 proteins  that have been implicated in adhesions  in this diagram so these are highly  complex and organized structures  that we need to further understand  so the goal is to identify and track the  adhesions at the leading edge so this is  a  these are areas that are protruding and  you can see these dynamic adhesions  this is a zoom in of this area here and  we used a marath surfaces feature in  order to identify these adhesions  track them over time and measure their  intensities  and from those intensities we can then  measure the dynamics of the adhesions  and  understand that those dynamics are  playing a role in regulating some  migration  with a quantified adhesion dynamics we  export the intensity data to excel  we have matlab code that was custom  designed  in the lab by alex keepass who was the  lead on this project  and were able to identify disassembly  stability and assembly events as the  adhesions  grow and disassemble and change over  time and  from these different events we can look  at a  semi-log plot of the data and calculate  the rate of disassembly and the rate of  assembly  from those plots  so this data shows lpp  each spot is a rate  for a single event so it could be an  adhesion they assemble and disassemble  so that would be  two spots on the curve an adhesion they  assemble and stabilize and then further  assemble that would be two assembly  events  so each dot here is an individual event  and if we look at just  control conditions we can measure the  assembly and disassembly and you see  this large distribution  of rape these measurements were done  with paxillin  as the readout of the adhesion so it's  not looking at lpp directly but at  excellent as a readout  of the adhesion  so when the cells are treated with tgf  beta what we see is an increase in both  the rate of assembly and the rate of  disassembly  so in general the adhesions are much  more dynamic  if we look at cells where ltp has been  knocked down  then they can't respond to the tgf beta  so similar to cell migration  the dynamics of the adhesion is not  increased  if we take those knockdown cells and  rescue them with gfp  lpp wild type then we can actually  regain the phenotype  and the cells can respond again to tgf  data  so we know that lpp is playing a role  in regulating the adhesion dynamics we  wanted to explore the protein in more  detail  so we have a mutant um lppm1  which is mutated in the m1 domain so  that it can't localize the focal  adhesion  and we have a second mutant delta abd  that has a mutation in the alpha actin  and binding domain  so that lpp can't link the adhesion to  the cytoskeleton  so when we go back to the cell migration  experiments you can see here  when we rescue the cells with lpp wild  type we see that the  from the red curves here that after  growth after treatment the