e much higher  tension  so the lower fret and higher tension so  the red here corresponds to high tension  low fret  and the blue to low tension for high fat  the treatment with growth factor causes  an increase in tension  but is this dependent on lpp so we have  our control  knockdown cells and then we have control  knockdown cells treated with tgf beta  we see a decrease in the fret ratio  which means an  increase in tension as i just showed you  with the tgf beta  above but then if we have the lpp  knocked down we don't see this change  and we don't see a response and a change  in tension in the adhesions  after growth factor treatment these bars  are an average of the tension  per adhesion if we look at the average  over each shell  we see the same phenotype that we see a  decrease in the fret ratio or an  increase in tension  in the control cells but when lpp is  knocked down we don't see that  changing tension these experiments  were done by an undergraduate in the lab  working with alex kupass  alex zawakowski  so if we look at images corresponding to  that data i just showed you  you can see in the control cells the lpp  knockdown cells  and the lpp knockdown cells with tgf  beta that we have a lot of blue  adhesions so these are corresponding to  low tension  or high fret whereas in the tgf beta  treated cells  in the presence of lpp you can see a lot  more adhesions that have red  or green yellow moderate to high tension  so we're seeing those cells are able to  respond to  the glow factor whereas the lpp  knockdown cells do not  so can that phenotype be rescued with  the delta abd lpp  so again we can see with the wild type  cells if we have the knockdown cells and  we put in  egfp wild types similar to the  endogenous we see a decrease in the  front ratio or an increase in tension  whereas when we try to rescue with the  delta abd lcp  for the lpp that can't find the actin  cytoskeleton  you can see that we don't see a response  and we actually see a slight  increase in the front ratio or decrease  in tension  which does show some statistical  significance although  we're working on getting some more data  for that condition  so basically the lpp has to be able to  bind to the actin cytoskeleton  in order for the adhesions to respond to  the  growth factor and have more tension  so another factor that that plays a role  in the progression of cancer  is the different tissues with different  biomechanical properties  so as i mentioned earlier women do not  die from breast cancer  they die from the metastatic cancer and  you can see from these numbers here that  at the time of death there's quite a  high rate of  cancer metastasis in the bone the liver  belongs in the brain  these tissues have very different  biomechanical properties so bone is very  hard and stiff  whereas brain is quite soft at about one  kilopascal  of uh sorry one pascal and one  kilopascal  of um stiffness and do these different  stiffnesses affect  the ability of breast cancer to  metastasize in those tissues  so it is known from fibroblast  mathematical models  and experiments that fibroblasts have a  certain stiffness where they move  optimally with the maximal speed and if  the surfaces are soft or hard the cells  do not move  as efficiently so we developed an  essay with the alan erlicker lab in  bioengineering at mcgill  using pdms substrates so these are a  polymer gel  and it can be very highly manipulated in  order to get very  defined stiffnesses of surface and then  the cells are plated on those surfaces  and the cell migration was measured  so if you look at the green curve here  you can see that when we  put lpp wild type in the knockdown cells  so lppgfp  we see something very similar to the  fibroblast where the cells move  optimally on about 40 kilopascal  surfaces  and on soft and hard surfaces they move  much more slowly  we also found that when we treat with  growth factor the cells can respond and  move faster  and this was independent of the  stiffness so we still see the general  trend with tgf beta  but the cells in general move faster on  all surfaces  however if the lpp is knocked down the  cells no longer sense the stiffness of  the surface  and they move at the same speed on all  of the surfaces  but taken together all of this data  really suggests that ltp is acting as a  mechanosensor  it's required for tgf beta induced  increases in cell migration and  inhibition dynamics  it's required for the formation of high  tension adaptations in response to  growth factor  it needs to be localized to the adhesion  and bind to the actin cytoskeleton for  these functions  and if we use the delta lpp delta abd  mutant which can't bind a reactant  cytoskeleton  the cells are unable to respond to the  growth factor  and lpp is required to sense and respond  to substrates with different mechanical  properties  so we're also interested in even in beta  podia so so far i've talked to you about  cell movement until migration but the  cells have to invade  surrounding tissues in order to leave  the breast  tissue and they need to invade tissues  at sites of metastasis  so the primary structure known to play a  role in this process  is the invadopodia and this structure  pushes  down into the tissue matrix metallic  proteases can digest the tissue  and then the active cytoskeleton plays a  role in creating  a force in order to push through the  tissue  and allow the cell to invade  but the question was is lpp involved in  this process  so for this part of the project a recent  masters student graduate from the lab  elena vurand who was in my lab as well  as  dr sakosam i developed this assay  using gelatin so basically what you do  is you  put down a fluorescent gelatin layer on  a  microscope cover glass and you have a  fluorophore on that gelatin so you have  a nice uniform layer and when you put  the cells down on that surface  if they undergo invasion then they  digest and push out of the way 