s that on very hard  surfaces there's a lot of invasions  on moderately stiff surfaces not so much  and on soft surfaces  somewhere in the intermediate again we  quantified that over a number of  different experiments with 154 fields of  view  and we saw something uh almost the  reverse of what we saw with migration  that the invasion was very high on stiff  surfaces and very high on soft surfaces  but in the intermediate surfaces the  invasion was much slower  so if we combine those two pieces of  data then what we find is that cell  migration is maximized on  moderately stiff or intermediate  stiffness around 40  kilopascal whereas invasion is higher on  soft surfaces  and hard surfaces so it's very um  possible that lpp is playing a role in  regulating  that when the surface is a intermediate  stiffness the cells are going to move  but if it's soft or hard they're going  to focus on invading the tissue  so i'm just going to give you one last  experiment that was really quite  interesting and it's it's preliminary  but  i thought it was quite interesting and  wanted to present it here today  so we looked at biophysical conditioning  of the cells and this was done  with the sql lab so what they did is  they  inject breast cancer cells into the  brain  of mice and leave them for three weeks  and then following the three weeks the  cells are isolated  and they're either put directly on  surfaces to look at invasions so this is  called exvival  or they're taken for one week and  conditioned on a plastic surface so a  relatively hard  plastic surface and we have what we call  ex-vivo plastic  at the same time we have another set of  the same cells that were not put into  the mouse  that are left in tissue culture on  plastic for the entire duration  and then imaged in the same assay  so the acids as i've just shown a few  slides ago  where we have a pdmf surface of  different stiffness on a glass coverslip  and then we have the gelatin layer  on which the cells are plated  so this figure is fairly busy but i'll  walk you through it and i think you'll  get the the general idea  so when we take the x-vivo cells the  um cells are not very invasive at all so  these cells have come out of very soft  brain tissue the brain tissue is around  one kilopascal  and the cells are not very invasive at  all when we do put them on a glass  surface  with um with the pdm without pdms sorry  just directly on the glass we put the  gelatin  then we we do see quite a bit of  invasiveness  however if the cells are put on on the  plastic dishes for a week and  reconditioned on this hard surface  then they tend to be invasive on all  surfaces of all stiffness  similarly the cells that were on the  plastic dish that were never put into  the mouth  are highly invasive on all surfaces  so this data was quantified and i'll  just mention again this is preliminary  data so it's a single  experimental run and i just want to  point out that the scaling here is quite  different on the three graphs  just for your information so the x vivo  cells really don't invade much at all  the  the total surface area degraded is only  going up to a 3000 here  although they do form invadopodia on the  glass surface  this is around an average of about 800  surface area the xvivoplastic you can  see is  is going up to 1500 so this is  significantly higher  and you can see a very similar trend to  it i showed you with the  other cell line that the cells tend to  invade maximally on soft and hard  surfaces  and are a little bit less invasive on  moderate stiffness  if you look at the cells that stayed on  the hard plastic surface for the entire  duration you can see the scale here is  50  000 so these are much more invasive than  the other two  conditions and you can see that the the  levels are quite high  across the um the difference thicknesses  we don't see the same clear trend that  we see here at the ex  vivoplastic but we're working on uh  doing the  experiment a few more times to validate  that  so overall um this shows that cells can  be conditioned so by moving to different  environments  they adapt to the environment they're in  and by being on a hard surface they tend  to  be able to adapt in order to invade  surfaces  of all different biomechanical  properties  so i just want to wrap up with another  short story  which is not related to breast cancer  but i think is  very important for all life cell imaging  and you'll notice in the whole breast  cancer project we did a lot of  live imaging under all the different  conditions and  we've been working on live imaging and  photo toxicity in my lab for quite a  long time  and recently discovered an issue that  we were aware of but not really aware of  the magnitude of the problem  so this work was recently published in  the journal of health science  and you can read all the details there i  just want to give you a little bit of a  snapshot  because i think this problem is probably  quite um  ubiquitous and i think most people are  not aware of it so  i think it's important  so when we do fluorescence imaging and  including the experiments that i've  shown you  here on focal adhesions and gelatin and  degradation and so on  there's a process we have to go through  so we need to open the shutter to expose  the sample to light  we need to turn on the light source  expose the sample to light and collect  an image  and then we have to turn off the light  source and or close the shutter  save the image and then repeat the  process so as we do the time lapse  imaging we  go around this circuit multiple times so  the key steps are  are opening the shutter turning on the  light source and then  closing the shutter and turning off the  light source and all shuttering is not  the same  so you can have a mechanical shredder if  you have like a mercury  a xenon or a metal halide source the  source stays on all the time and you  have a mechanical shutter that opens and  closes  to exposure sample to light for  fluorescence imaging  or you can have electronic control so  with led light sources you can have usb  control from the image acquisition  software or you can have direct ttl  electronic triggering from the camera  and as i mentioned it's really important  to know that all shuttering is not the  same  but the reason this problem came to our  attention is we were working on some  experiments to systematically look at  how the  incident light power of the lamp and the  exposure time  were playing a role in phototoxicity if  at all  and what we found is when we use high  incident light power  and short exposure time so 24  milliseconds here  the cells were not very dynamic at all  so these rose plots are showing fairly  short  um cell tracks however when we use the  long exposure time with low light power  we did find that the cells moved quite  significantly and these matched up  really well with our bright field  imaging  where we wouldn't expect any sort of  toxicity so initially we thought that  the reason  the the cells were not moving while at  high power was just because of the power  of the light  so the cells were seeing a lot of  photons in a short amount of time  but we found out after that that was not  the case  so we used an oscilloscope to actually  measure the shuttering  of our light source and we had three  different settings that we looked at  so in all cases the software was set for  a 24 millisecond camera exposure  and in the first case the light was  turned on and off using a ttl  trigger so this is a cable which  basically goes directly from the camera  to the light source  and sends a signal from the camera  saying i'm ready to collect  light and collect an image and in this  case we had no what we call illumination  overhead  the shutter really opened and closed or  the lamp turned on and off  in 24 milliseconds however in the  experiment i just showed you we had been  using the usb connection because that  gives us the ability  to control the light source using the  image acquisition software and what we  found there is this red curve  and when we were actually doing a 24  millisecond image acquisition  the light was actually on for 137  milliseconds or almost six times the  exposure time  if you're using a mechanical shutter  this is a physical shutter that needs to  open and close  and in that case we found it took about  230 milliseconds  or 10 times the exposure time so these  delays are termed  illumination overhead where the sample  is exposed to light  but the detector is not collecting  an image and  these these delays are linked both to  software and hardware  so in the mechanical shutter it  physically has to open and close  in the usb light we know that the led  can turn on very rapidly  so there must be other potential  software delays  playing a role there so we would factor  a cell migration experiment and this is  the data i already showed you where at  high incident light power the cells  don't move very well  and when we were sure that the sample  was being  illuminated with the ttl triggering so  that it was only illuminated for 24  milliseconds for each image  then we did see cell tracks that were  very similar to the  long exposure time low light levels so  the effect that we were seeing was  actually due to this illumination  overhead  in this case we had the usb signaling  going and so there was some kind of  software delay that was allowing the  light to stay on  about six times longer  so this is another example just showing  you what can happen in the lysol  experiment  so on the left side we have low power  and long exposure time  and we're looking at cells expressing  paxil and gfp  also stained with mitre tracker red and  you can see that the cells are very  dynamic  and the mitochondria are moving around  and fusing  and diffusing and everything looks quite  good this is four hours of imaging  taking an image every minute  and taking an image of the mitre tra