ll need to disassemble  in order to release the attachment and  allow the whole cell to process forward  so we're interested in understanding  these different structures  and their dynamics  so there's also three-dimensional  organization so this is a cartoon of an  individual one of these structures so if  we look  it would be one of these single  structures in the south and the  molecular organization there  so we have um the integrin which is  linking  the extracellular matrix to the  cytoplasmic side of the cell these are  transmembrane proteins  we have an integrand signaling layer  this is where paxillin and a number of  other proteins are  kinases phosphatases things that are  regulated and controlling downstream  signaling in the cell there's a forced  transduction layer which consists of a  lot of structural proteins such as  vinculin and talin that are linking  the adhesion to the actin cytoskeleton  and then there's an actin regulatory  layer which contains proteins such as  vixen and we believe  lpp would be in this layer since it's in  the vixen family  and just to mention that we're only  showing nine of the over 200 proteins  that have been implicated in adhesions  in this diagram so these are highly  complex and organized structures  that we need to further understand  so the goal is to identify and track the  adhesions at the leading edge so this is  a  these are areas that are protruding and  you can see these dynamic adhesions  this is a zoom in of this area here and  we used a marath surfaces feature in  order to identify these adhesions  track them over time and measure their  intensities  and from those intensities we can then  measure the dynamics of the adhesions  and  understand that those dynamics are  playing a role in regulating some  migration  with a quantified adhesion dynamics we  export the intensity data to excel  we have matlab code that was custom  designed  in the lab by alex keepass who was the  lead on this project  and were ab