e much higher  tension  so the lower fret and higher tension so  the red here corresponds to high tension  low fret  and the blue to low tension for high fat  the treatment with growth factor causes  an increase in tension  but is this dependent on lpp so we have  our control  knockdown cells and then we have control  knockdown cells treated with tgf beta  we see a decrease in the fret ratio  which means an  increase in tension as i just showed you  with the tgf beta  above but then if we have the lpp  knocked down we don't see this change  and we don't see a response and a change  in tension in the adhesions  after growth factor treatment these bars  are an average of the tension  per adhesion if we look at the average  over each shell  we see the same phenotype that we see a  decrease in the fret ratio or an  increase in tension  in the control cells but when lpp is  knocked down we don't see that  changing tension these experiments  were done by an undergraduate in the lab  working with alex kupass  alex zawakowski  so if we look at images corresponding to  that data i just showed you  you can see in the control cells the lpp  knockdown cells  and the lpp knockdown cells with tgf  beta that we have a lot of blue  adhesions so these are corresponding to  low tension  or high fret whereas in the tgf beta  treated cells  in the presence of lpp you can see a lot  more adhesions that have red  or green yellow moderate to high tension  so we're seeing those cells are able to  respond to  the glow factor whereas the lpp  knockdown cells do not  so can that phenotype be rescued with  the delta abd lpp  so again we can see with the wild type  cells if we have the knockdown cells and  we put in  egfp wild types similar to the  endogenous we see a decrease in the  front ratio or an increase in tension  whereas when we try to rescue with the  delta abd lcp  for the lpp that can't find the actin  cytoskeleton  you can see that we don't see a response  and we actually see a slight  increase in the front ratio or decrease  in tension  which does show some statistical  significance although  we're working on getting some more data  for that condition  so basically the lpp has to be able to  bind to the actin cytoskeleton  in order for the adhesions to respond to  the  growth factor and have more tension  so another factor that that plays a role  in the progression of cancer  is the different tissues with different  biomechanical properties  so as i mentioned earlier women do not  die from breast cancer  they die from the metastatic cancer and  you can see from these numbers here that  at the time of death there's quite a  high rate of  cancer metastasis in the bone the liver  belongs in the brain  these tissues have very different  biomechanical properties so bone is very  hard and stiff  whereas brain is quite soft at about one  kilopascal  of uh sorry one pascal and one  kilopascal  of um stiffness and do these different  stiffnesses affect  the ability of breast cancer to  metastasize in those tissues  so it is known from fibroblast  mathematical models  and experiments that fibroblasts have a  certain stiffness where they move  optimally with the maximal speed and if  the surfaces are soft or hard the cells  do not move  as efficiently so we developed an  essay with the alan erlicker lab in  bioengineering at mcgill  using pdms substrates so these are a  polymer gel  and it can be very highly manipulated in  order to get very  defined stiffnesses of surface and then  the cells are plated on those surfaces  and the cell migration was measured  so if you look at the green curve here  you can see that when we  put lpp wild type in the knockdown cells  so lppgfp  we see something very similar to the  fibroblast where the cells move  optimally on about 40 kilopascal  surfaces  and on soft and hard surfaces they move  much more slowly  we also found that when we treat with  growth factor the cells can respond and  move faster  and this was independent of the  stiffness so we still see the general  trend with tgf beta  but the cells in general move faster on  all surfaces  however if the lpp is knocked down the  cells no longer sense the stiffness of  the surface  and they move at the same speed on all  of the surfaces  but taken together all of this data  really suggests that ltp is acting as a  mechanosensor  it's required for tgf beta induced  increases in cell migration and  inhibition dynamics  it's required for the formation of high  tension adaptations in response to  growth factor  it needs to be localized to the adhesion  and bind to the actin cytoskeleton for  these functions  and if we use the delta lpp delta abd  mutant which can't bind a reactant  cytoskeleton  the cells are unable to respond to the  growth factor  and lpp is required to sense and respond  to substrates with different mechanical  properties  so we're also interested in even in beta  podia so so far i've talked to you about  cell movement until migration but the  cells have to invade  surrounding tissues in order to leave  the breast  tissue and they need to invade tissues  at sites of metastasis  so the primary structure known to play a  role in this process  is the invadopodia and this structure  pushes  down into the tissue matrix metallic  proteases can digest the tissue  and then the active cytoskeleton plays a  role in creating  a force in order to push through the  tissue  and allow the cell to invade  but the question was is lpp involved in  this process  so for this part of the project a recent  masters student graduate from the lab  elena vurand who was in my lab as well  as  dr sakosam i developed this assay  using gelatin so basically what you do  is you  put down a fluorescent gelatin layer on  a  microscope cover glass and you have a  fluorophore on that gelatin so you have  a nice uniform layer and when you put  the cells down on that surface  if they undergo invasion then they  digest and push out of the way  and break down the gelatin so that you  get dark  areas on a bright background and this uh  these  surfaces can be characterized again we  use the surface feature and amaris in  order to do that  so you can see here the actin staining  so it's effective fluid and staining  lcp and focal adhesions and then you can  see the  invasion spots from  these two cells  so again we looked at the impact of tgf  data on invasions so  in the control cells we do see some  invasive spots  but when we treat with tgf data the  cells are significantly more invasive  if we quantify that you can see that the  cells without growth factor treatment  are much less in beta invasive than  following  growth factor treatment and this was a  challenging assay and challenging to  quantify as well  so we settled on looking at the total  surface area degraded per field of view  so we tried to find areas that have  similar numbers of cells  and look at the percentage of the area  that field of view  that was dark  so is lpp important for this so this is  showing another example  of cells expressing lpp where we have  minus  m plus tgf beta and again you can see  that increase in invasiveness with the  growth factor  and you can also see that here in the  quantification  however if we put in the lpp knockdown  cells  the cells are unable to respond to the  growth factor and the invasiveness does  not increase  so it looks like that invasion and  degradation of the gelatin  in response to growth factor is  dependent on the presence of lpp  so you're interested in combining the  invasion assay with the  biomechanics or substrate stiffness so  in this experiment we have the glass  coverslip  we put the pdms layer similar to the  cell migration experiments  and then we add gelatin on top so we  have the three layers  and we can look at the degradation of  the gelatin on surfaces of different  stiffness  so here you can see the gelatin stain  the actin staining and then the overlay  and what we found is that on very hard  surfaces there's a lot of invasions  on moderately stiff surfaces not so much  and on soft surfaces  somewhere in the intermediate again we  quantified that over a number of  different experiments with 154 fields of  view  and we saw something uh almost the  reverse of what we saw with migration  that the invasion was very high on stiff  surfaces and very high on soft surfaces  but in the intermediate surfaces the  invasion was much slower  so if we combine those two pieces of  data then what we find is that cell  migration is maximized on  moderately stiff or intermediate  stiffness around 40  kilopascal whereas invasion is higher on  soft surfaces  and hard surfaces so it's very um  possible that lpp is playing a role in  regulating  that when the surface is a intermediate  stiffness the cells are going to move  but if it's soft or hard they're going  to focus on invading the tissue  so i'm just going to give you one last  experiment that was really quite  interesting and it's it's preliminary  but  i thought it was quite interesting and  wanted to present it here today  so we looked at biophysical conditioning  of the cells and this was done  with the sql lab so what they did is  they  inject breast cancer cells into the  brain  of mice and leave them for three weeks  and then following the three weeks the  cells are isolated  and they're either put directly on  surfaces to look at invasions so this is  called exvival  or they're taken for one week and  conditioned on a plastic surface so a  relatively hard  plastic surface and we have what we call  ex-vivo plastic  at the same time we have another set of  the same cells that were not put into  the mouse  that are left in tissue culture on  plastic for the entire duration  and then imaged in the same assay  so the acids as i've just shown a few  slides ago  where we have a pdmf surface of  different stiffness on a glass coverslip  and then we have the gelatin layer  on which the cells are plated  so this figure is fairly busy but i'll  walk you through it and i think you'll  get the the general idea  so when we take the x-vivo cells the  um cells are not very invasive at all so  these cells have come out of very soft  brain tissue the brain tissue is around  one kilopascal  and the cells are not very invasive at  all when we do put them on a glass  surface  with um with the pdm without pdms sorry  just directly on the glass we put the  gelatin  then we we do see quite a bit of  invasiveness  however if the cells are put on on the  plastic dishes for a week and  reconditioned on this hard surface  then they tend to be invasive on all  surfaces of all stiffness  similarly the cells that were on the  plastic dish that were never put into  the mouth  are highly invasive on all surfaces  so this data was quantified and i'll  just mention again this is preliminary  data so it's a single  experimental run and i just want to  point out that the scaling here is quite  different on the three graphs  just for your information so the x vivo  cells really don't invade much at all  the  the total surface area degraded is only  going up to a 3000 here  although they do form invadopodia on the  glass surface  this is around an average of about 800  surface area the xvivoplastic you can  see is  is going up to 1500 so this is  significantly higher  and you can see a very similar trend to  it i showed you with the  other cell line that the cells tend to  invade maximally on soft and hard  surfaces  and are a little bit less invasive on  moderate stiffness  if you look at the cells that stayed on  the hard plastic surface for the entire  duration you can see the scale here is  50  000 so these are much more invasive than  the other two  conditions and you can see that the the  levels are quite high  across the um the difference thicknesses  we don't see the same clear trend that  we see here at the ex  vivoplastic but we're working on uh  doing the  experiment a few more times to validate  that  so overall um this shows that cells can  be conditioned so by moving to different  environments  they adapt to the environment they're in  and by being on a hard surface they tend  to  be able to adapt in order to invade  surfaces  of all different biomechanical  properties  so i just want to wrap up with another  short story  which is not related to breast cancer  but i think is  very important for all life cell imaging  and you'll notice in the whole breast  cancer project we did a lot of  live imaging under all the different  conditions and  we've been working on live imaging and  photo toxicity in my lab for quite a  long time  and recently discovered an issue that  we were aware of but not really aware of  the magnitude of the problem  so this work was recently published in  the journal of health science  and you can read all the details there i  just want to give you a little bit of a  snapshot  because i think this problem is probably  quite um  ubiquitous and i think most people are  not aware of it so  i think it's important  so when we do fluorescence imaging and  including the experiments that i've  shown you  here on focal adhesions and gelatin and  degradation and so on  there's a process we have to go through  so we need to open the shutter to expose  the sample to light  we need to turn on the light source  expose the sample to light and collect  an image  and then we have to turn off the light  source and or close the shutter  save the image and then repeat the  process so as we do the time lapse  imaging we  go around this circuit multiple times so  the key steps are  are opening the shutter turning on the  light source and then  closing the shutter and turning off the  light source and all shuttering is not  the same  so you can have a mechanical shredder if  you have like a mercury  a xenon or a metal halide source the  source stays on all the time and you  have a mechanical shutter that opens and  closes  to exposure sample to light for  fluorescence imaging  or you can have electronic control so  with led light sources you can have usb  control from the image acquisition  software or you can have direct ttl  electronic triggering from the camera  and as i mentioned it's really important  to know that all shuttering is not the  same  but the reason this problem came to our  attention is we were working on some  experiments to systematically look at  how the  incident light power of the lamp and the  exposure time  were playing a role in phototoxicity if  at all  and what we found is when we use high  incident light power  and short exposure time so 24  milliseconds here  the cells were not very dynamic at all  so these rose plots are showing fairly  short  um cell tracks however when we use the  long exposure time with low light power  we did find that the cells moved quite  significantly and these matched up  really well with our bright field  imaging  where we wouldn't expect any sort of  toxicity so initially we thought that  the reason  the the cells were not moving while at  high power was just because of the power  of the light  so the cells were seeing a lot of  photons in a short amount of time  but we found out after that that was not  the case  so we used an oscilloscope to actually  measure the shuttering  of our light source and we had three  different settings that we looked at  so in all cases the software was set for  a 24 millisecond camera exposure  and in the first case the light was  turned on and off using a ttl  trigger so this is a cable which  basically goes directly from the camera  to the light source  and sends a signal from the camera  saying i'm ready to collect  light and collect an image and in this  case we had no what we call illumination  overhead  the shutter really opened and closed or  the lamp turned on and off  in 24 milliseconds however in the  experiment i just showed you we had been  using the usb connection because that  gives us the ability  to control the light source using the  image acquisition software and what we  found there is this red curve  and when we were actually doing a 24  millisecond image acquisition  the light was actually on for 137  milliseconds or almost six times the  exposure time  if you're using a mechanical shutter  this is a physical shutter that needs to  open and close  and in that case we found it took about  230 milliseconds  or 10 times the exposure time so these  delays are termed  illumination overhead where the sample  is exposed to light  but the detector is not collecting  an image and  these these delays are linked both to  software and hardware  so in the mechanical shutter it  physically has to open and close  in the usb light we know that the led  can turn on very rapidly  so there must be other potential  software delays  playing a role there so we would factor  a cell migration experiment and this is  the data i already showed you where at  high incident light power the cells  don't move very well  and when we were sure that the sample  was being  illuminated with the ttl triggering so  that it was only illuminated for 24  milliseconds for each image  then we did see cell tracks that were  very similar to the  long exposure time low light levels so  the effect that we were seeing was  actually due to this illumination  overhead  in this case we had the usb signaling  going and so there was some kind of  software delay that was allowing the  light to stay on  about six times longer  so this is another example just showing  you what can happen in the lysol  experiment  so on the left side we have low power  and long exposure time  and we're looking at cells expressing  paxil and gfp  also stained with mitre tracker red and  you can see that the cells are very  dynamic  and the mitochondria are moving around  and fusing  and diffusing and everything looks quite  good this is four hours of imaging  taking an image every minute  and taking an image of the mitre tracker  every six minutes  on the right side we have the high power  short exposure time  and this was collected with the usb  signaling so we know that we have about  five times the amount of light  on the sample then we expect i'm sure  almost six times  and you can see here that the  mitochondria very early on start to  condense and the cell starts to round up  and eventually  the cell likely dies and disappears from  the field of view  you can see the importance of of this  illumination overhead and how it can  really drastically affect the  phototoxicity  so the best thing to do is trigger your  light source directly with the ttl  and this is only possible if you have a  solid state source so an led based light  source or a solid state laser  you can also measure the elimination  overhead because we found there's all  kinds of differences depending on the  exposure time you said  and the frame rate even the delay  between images can affect  the elimination overhead and for  bulb-based systems where you have to use  a mechanical shutter and you can't do  direct triggering  it's best to use the lower power and  longer exposure time  that would be similar to this condition  so that you can image yourselves for a  long period of time  without the effect of toxicity  so as i mentioned that's just a bit of a  snapshot and all the details are in  the publication but i really think it's  important that people are aware of this  phenomena  and adapt their lifestyle imaging  conditions for it  we observed it also in our spinning disc  confocal  which had a software delay as well even  though it had solid state layers  laser sorry so i'd like to thank  the members of my lab i mentioned the  people who worked on the project as i  was going along  and also the advanced bio imaging  facility staff all of the  imaging was done in our facility at  mcgill  so i mentioned the students in my lab  our collaborators  peter siegel is really the lab that  initiated the ltp project  and i've been really excited working  with them  trying to get at the molecular level and  look at live cell imaging  and alan erlicker's lab has worked with  us for all of the  pdms and substrate stiffness experiments  and thank you of course to all of our  funding agencies  without them this work wouldn't be  possible and i thank you for your  attention and  look forward to answering any questions  hi claire that was a great talk  um i'm so glad that you shared the  the work you guys did looking at the  photo toxicity i think it's such an  important story for everyone to hear  and those early results are super  tantalizing  um so yes thank you again  um i uh i do have a  uh some questions um when you're in the  audience  good i do have some questions from um  the audience  so let's get started with those  a lot of the work that you showed uh was  of cells migrating in 2d does this  have relevance in vivo  so um i know a lot of uh there's a lot  of debate in the field about about  the experiments that are done on on flat  glass surfaces but there are a lot of  surfaces in the biology  um including like the basement membrane  sometimes cells will move along  blood vessels so that's kind of like a  rolling or folded  two-dimensional surface and even  movement along collagen fibers people  have modeled as more of a  one-dimensional migration where the  cells are moving  along almost like a rope or a cable  so i do think it has some role to play  in  in the natural environment of the cell  okay yeah i hadn't thought about  collagen  as a as a 1d thing that's cool  um sort of along the same lines do you  have plans to explore the role of lpp  and regulating cell migration and more  physiological  uh environments so we have um  some data where we've been looking at  the cells on top of  uh three-dimensional surfaces so rather  than using the pdms which is  really a chemical uh material that we've  that you know that has been developed  we put the cells on top of things like  bovine collagen or  rat tail collagen that are that are more  physiological relevant  and we are working on assays now to look  at single cells  and spheroids in three-dimensional  gels the um sample prep and so on is  actually quite challenging to get  reproducibility and  and have the cells moving and and  migrating  in a systematic way and then the  microscopy in that case we're working  with both spinning discs and  light sheet imaging so we can image fast  enough  to see the processes  you mentioned pdms um  how how hard is it to make  all those diff the series of different  stiffnesses and the  in the pdms um  so we did that in collaboration with  alan erlicker's group and  some really tremendous bioengineers in  his lab  um it's super tricky and  alanna has graduated so i'm hoping that  we'll be able to maintain it in the lab  but it's um yeah it's a lot of trial and  error there's a lot of chemical steps  some of the steps for cleaning the  surface there's a reagent called piranha  so of course you want to make sure you  don't have any piranha left by the time  you put your cells down  we had issues with drying out and  cracking and then trying to put the  gelatin  on top you you have a another interface  um where the gelatin can peel off or or  you know wrap over on itself and so on  so  um so i think we have it down to a  pretty good protocol but i think  you know kudos to the students for  persisting because it  uh the results are fabulous but the the  troubleshooting is pretty intense so  yeah we wouldn't know it from what  you've shown  um let's see so  another another thing is that there's  you're showing a lot of data on  individual cells or individual adhesions  do you have a plan to perform more  advanced  data analytics really rather than just  looking at averages  so this is a direction my lab's really  been moving to  in the last i would say year or so i  have a post-doc now who's a  computer scientist who's got a lot of  expertise in deep learning and machine  learning  and we're starting to apply things like  unsupervised learning with  like hierarchical clustering and  bioinformatics that are used  more in genomics but are starting to be  applied more in imaging  and uh it's really fascinating um we're  seeing like  you know you might have a treatment and  90 of the cells respond but there's just  10  that don't and now we're interested like  what's going on with that 10 percent  and i guess eventually um it's not  really my area of expertise but you  could also imagine  doing like single cell rna-seq and and  really trying to tease out how come  those cells  you know respond differently and i am  was at a seminar years ago and and uh it  was a really cute story that the speaker  talked about uh school buses and that if  you were  looking from space and studying school  buses on the earth you would conclude  that they aggregate in big groups  and they don't move and unless you  actually happen  you know at six in the morning or three  in the afternoon you'd never see that  and so it's always kind of stuck with me  that  maybe the the things we're always seeing  are not the most interesting so  yeah yeah oh that's cool i look forward  to  hearing more about all of that um and  i want to make sure that we have a  question talking about  the the second half for the second part  um of what you presented today  so um you were talking about  illumination  overhead and this concept of making sure  you're thinking about  all of the illumination that's going to  the sample and  so how big of a problem or how how broad  of a problem do you think this  is for everyone so we were super  surprised i was  pretty sure that this was due to the  light power  and i'd always thought that there's just  more photons hitting the cell really  quickly  and i didn't i knew that there was  issues with the timing but i didn't  really think it was  the only thing going on and it really  seems from our data that it's  the major effect is is from this effect  and it's really complicated we've done  more experiments  and we find it on spinning discs so even  though our  our laser is triggered um electronically  it's a solid state laser we still have  the 17 millisecond delay between images  that we can't really explain  and so if you're working with high  intensity and short exposure times that  17 milliseconds is really significant  um so what our approach now is we're  actually working on a protocol paper on  how to measure  it because it took us a long time to  figure out how to measure it properly  and then i think one of the things i'd  really like to do is just bring  awareness to people  because i think most people don't know  about it and there are fixes so once you  know it's there  it's a lot easier to fix it so i think  we've been trying to talk to the  microscope  vendors and core facilities and just get  the word out  um to just be aware of this if you're  doing uh  live imaging but that being said i think  it's also important for fixed cells you  can imagine if you're doing  high-resolution z-stack and trying to do  deconvolution it's photobleaching as  you're going  but you're really you know throwing away  all this light which you don't have to  be if you  if you're um you know more in tune with  us  um we had another question come in  um given what you've learned about the  light dosing are you trying to account  for that when  imaging through the pdms and the gelatin  because they have different optical  properties  um so it's it is tricky imaging through  those  the experiments i showed you with the  the pdms and the gelatin are actually  done on laser scanning confocal because  we have to  have the signal to noise through that  thick surface so they're actually  difficult to do on a wide field  so usually with laser scanning confocal  you don't have to worry about the  overhead illumination because  these are triggered instantaneously  pretty much with  acoustic optics but i do think the power  density then starts to play a role  because you've got the light focused  into this little tiny spot  with a lot of photons so you do have to  play around a bit with scan speed  and intensity of the light and and so on  to be sure that  you're minimizing photo toxicity on that  platform as well  okay okay i think we'll wrap up with  questions but  um there's one other thing that we  wanted to talk about  claire has mentioned how she's  publishing and making sure that other  core facilities  and that sort of thing know about this  overhead problem and you're performing  what i see is a really nice service to  the community  and other ways that you are serving the  community is taking part in these  other organizations and so you along  with allison north are  co-chairs of the bio imaging north  america  group and i think you wanted to talk  just a little bit about that and make  sure everybody knows about it  so yeah i'll let you do that yeah so  it's it's a recent  group we founded it about two years ago  and  the idea is to just bring the community  together  to build consensus around different  things to build awareness  the focus of the group right now is  working groups so we have working groups  on quality control and standards  we're in the process of forming a new  group on image analysis  we have a diversity and inclusion group  and  we also have a communications group and  we're looking to  to form more as the the group grows and  the sort of the  um way that we've been forming those  working groups is getting people who are  not part of the executive  to take a lead and we've been bringing  in a lot of younger  researchers who are just starting up  core facilities and  and i think bringing building the  building the community and they're  bringing along  their peers and and so on and for  instance with the quality control and  standards group we're working on getting  a consensus  of how to measure quality control on a  comp vocal  and then we want to present that to the  community and try to come up with a  consensus on you know the best way to  measure resolution or  or laser stability and things like that  so i think it's going to be a tremendous  tool for the community  we also plan we've applied for funding  from the nsf  and we're hoping to get money which will  be focused  completely on core facility scientists  so  it'll be for travel to meetings for job  shadowing  when we can get back to traveling and um  and just uh again just building up that  expertise and really recognizing the  imaging scientists  who are working in facilities and and  helping you know  the whole community of of labs working  in  areas like cell and developmental  biology yes so  um so next week we actually have an  event um  three weeks ago we had an event looking  at personal protective equipment  and and how to open facilities safely  and  we've published those guidelines that  kind of came out of the meeting  on the royal microscopy society website  and we're having a follow-up meeting  next wednesday june 10th on  ways to manage users  and we're going to have some of the  the companies that are working with core  facility management software  i know for me i have like three or four  microscopes in some rooms so we're going  to have to make sure we don't have  too many people accessing the room at  the same time  how do we prioritize people and so on so  so that's next  wednesday in collaboration with uk bio  imaging and  and the raw microscopy society so i  would encourage people to to join in the  conversation and  we're all learning from each other so  anybody has any great ideas we'd love to  hear them  it's all new for all of us right so  yeah well thank you again for taking  part in that and really  sort of yeah providing  some really nice resources to the entire  community and i think everyone  does appreciate it and i very much  appreciate you  taking part in our meeting and  presenting this keynote  it was great um and thank you to  everyone  that has uh watched us and will watch us  so these will be around for a year um so  if you haven't been able to catch  a keynote or one of the other um  talks that's out there they will be  within this in virtual environment for a  whole year  um so you have some time and yeah  uh we'll make sure that the other  questions that came in from claire  came in for claire that she gets those  as well too so  thanks again to everyone thanks claire  thank you  take care everybody
 

